Now I understand the bug. Looking at the `processJob` method in `src/queue.ts`:

```typescript
async processJob(
    job: Job<T>,
    handler: (payload: T) => Promise<void> | void
  ): Promise<{ success: boolean; job: Job<T> }> {
    const current: Job<T> = { ...job, attempts: job.attempts + 1 };
    try {
      await handler(current.payload);
      return { success: true, job: current };
    } catch (err) {
      // BUG: swallows error and reports success
      return { success: true, job: current };
    }
  }
```

The bug is in the `catch` block: when an error is caught, it returns `{ success: true, ... }` instead of `{ success: false, ... }`. Also, looking at the test `processJob retry re-enqueues`, it expects that after a failure, the job is re-enqueued (q.size() === 1). But the current `processJob` doesn't re-enqueue failed jobs.

So there are two bugs:
1. The catch block returns `success: true` instead of `success: false`
2. Failed jobs are not re-enqueued when retries remain

Let me look at the test more carefully:

```typescript
test("processJob retry re-enqueues", async () => {
    const q = new AsyncQueue<string>({ retryDelay: 0 });
    const job = q.enqueue("fail", { maxAttempts: 3 });
    // dequeue first
    const dequeued = q.dequeue();
    let attempts = 0;
    const handler = () => {
      attempts++;
      throw new Error("fail");
    };
    const r1 = await q.processJob(dequeued, handler);
    expect(r1.success).toBe(false);
    expect(r1.job.attempts).toBe(1);
    expect(q.size()).toBe(1); // re-enqueued
```

So the test expects:


- `r1.success` to be `false` (currently returns `true`)
- After calling `processJob`, the queue should have 1 item (re-enqueued)

The fix needs to return `{ success: false, job: current }` in the catch block and re-enqueue the job if it has remaining attempts before the method completes.

